body:
A timeout with `fallback` fulfills with the fallback value without calling the input promise's `.cancel()`. With `message: false` and no fallback, the timeout calls `.cancel()` and fulfills with `undefined`.

The [README](https://github.com/sindresorhus/p-timeout/blob/8bbf53936a8e2fc5e1566cf399afbcbf53ddefb5/readme.md#ptimeoutinput-options) and [type documentation](https://github.com/sindresorhus/p-timeout/blob/8bbf53936a8e2fc5e1566cf399afbcbf53ddefb5/index.d.ts#L120-L125) describe cancellation on timeout without a fallback exception. The [fallback branch](https://github.com/sindresorhus/p-timeout/blob/8bbf53936a8e2fc5e1566cf399afbcbf53ddefb5/index.js#L57-L69) returns before the call to `.cancel()`.

Reproduced with unmodified source at `8bbf53936a8e2fc5e1566cf399afbcbf53ddefb5` (package version 7.0.2), Node.js v24.21.0, Linux x64. Save this as `repro.mjs` in the repository root. It uses the public `customTimers` option to trigger the timeout directly:

```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import pTimeout from './index.js';

async function observe(options) {
	let cancelCalls = 0;
	let rejectInput;
	let fire;
	const input = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
		rejectInput = reject;
	});
	input.cancel = () => {
		cancelCalls++;
		rejectInput(new Error('Input canceled'));
	};

	const result = pTimeout(input, {
		milliseconds: 1,
		...options,
		customTimers: {
			setTimeout(callback) {
				fire = callback;
				return 1;
			},
			clearTimeout() {},
		},
	});
	const outcome = result.then(
		value => ({status: 'fulfilled', value}),
		error => ({status: 'rejected', message: error.message}),
	);
	assert.equal(typeof fire, 'function');
	fire();
	return {...await outcome, cancelCalls};
}

const noFallback = await observe({message: false});
const withFallback = await observe({fallback: () => 'FALLBACK'});
console.log({noFallback, withFallback});

assert.deepEqual(noFallback, {status: 'fulfilled', value: undefined, cancelCalls: 1});
assert.equal(withFallback.status, 'fulfilled');
assert.equal(withFallback.value, 'FALLBACK');
assert.equal(withFallback.cancelCalls, 1, 'Fallback should cancel the input on timeout');
```

Run:

```sh
timeout --signal=TERM --kill-after=3s 30s node --unhandled-rejections=strict repro.mjs
```

Both results are printed and the control assertions pass before the final assertion fails with exit code 1. Relevant output, with the stack omitted:

```text
{
  noFallback: { status: 'fulfilled', value: undefined, cancelCalls: 1 },
  withFallback: { status: 'fulfilled', value: 'FALLBACK', cancelCalls: 0 }
}

AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Fallback should cancel the input on timeout

0 !== 1
```

I also checked `fallback: () => input` with the input resolved after the timeout: it currently fulfills successfully without calling `.cancel()`. Canceling the input before invoking the fallback would change that behavior.

Should every timeout cancel the input, including when `fallback` is provided, or should the documentation state that a fallback takes responsibility for cancellation? This seems worth clarifying before changing the behavior.

Upgrading from `6.1.4` to `7.0.0`, I get errors in my console:

```
index.js:88 Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: Illegal invocation
    at cancelablePromise.clear (index.js:88:16)
    at index.js:81:21
    ...
```

It seems the same error that #18 solved. I see [in the `6.1.4...7.0.0` diff](https://github.com/sindresorhus/p-timeout/compare/v6.1.4...v7.0.0) that `7.0.0` goes back to calling `customTimers.setTimeout` directly (instead of calling via `.call(undefined, ...)`), so I'm guessing that's the issue. No idea what's the correct approach, though.

#### Additional context

My usage of `pTimeout` is pretty vanilla:
```ts
const newCompiled = await pTimeout(
  // This function returns a promise.
  getCompilationOutput(
    valueModelRef.current.uri.toString(),
    forms.constants.COMPILATION_FAILED,
  ),
  {
    // Just a constant number.
    milliseconds: MONACO_EDITOR_COMPILATION_TIMEOUT,
    // A fallback returning a static string. Returning a promise to a string doesn't fix the error.
    fallback: () => forms.constants.COMPILATION_FAILED,
  },
);
```

